bool _Swap() {
    bool tmp = A;
    A = B;
    B = tmp;
    return B;
}
// initial A=B=FALSE
// Every process:
B = TRUE;
while (_Swap());
// CS
A = FALSE;

//---------------------------------------------------------------------

"Queue-Based Hierarchical Two-State Toggle Critical Section Synchronization"
// Every CS Have there own queue of Processes
bool _Swap() {
  A,B = B,A;
  PQ.enqueue(this.sleep(B), B); // sleep when TRUE
}                              // push when TRUE
                              



// Parent Thread             //
while(!PQ.empty){           //
  PQ.pop(!A).run() {       // pop when TRUE
    B = TRUE;
    _Swap();
    // C_Section
    A = FALSE;
    PQ.pop().weakup();
  }
}


________________________________________

Parent process
    |
    +-- Child 1
    |      |
    |      +-- CS_A + Queue_A
    |      +-- CS_B + Queue_B
    |
    +-- Child 2
           |
           +-- CS_A + Queue_A
           +-- CS_C + Queue_C

This algorithm proposes a queue-managed synchronization model where every critical section (CS) has its own dedicated waiting queue.
Instead of using a single global lock for all processes, each resource/critical section maintains an independent queue of processes that want access to it.
When a process requests a critical section:
1.	It tries to acquire access using an atomic swap operation.
2.	If the critical section is free, the process enters immediately.
3.	If the critical section is already occupied, the process is placed into that CS's waiting queue and sleeps.
4.	When the current process finishes, ownership is transferred to the next waiting process in the queue.
This avoids busy waiting because waiting processes sleep instead of continuously checking the lock.
The model is designed for systems where a parent process manages many child processes, and each child may have multiple sub-processes with different critical sections. 
Each critical section has its own synchronization queue, reducing contention between unrelated resources.
Structure:
                 Parent Process
                       |
        +--------------+--------------+
        |              |              |
      Child 1        Child 2        Child 3
        |              |              |
   +----+----+    +----+----+    +----+----+
   |         |    |         |    |         |
  CS-A      CS-B CS-C      CS-D CS-E      CS-F
   |         |    |         |    |         |
 Queue-A  Queue-B Queue-C Queue-D Queue-E Queue-F
   |
 Processes waiting
   |
 P1 -> P2 -> P3


Example:
CS-A Queue:
P1
P2
P3

CS-B Queue:
P4
P5

CS-C Queue:
P6
P7
P8
Each queue operates independently.
Goals:
•	Reduce lock contention
•	Avoid CPU waste from busy waiting
•	Allow independent critical sections to progress in parallel
•	Provide FIFO ordering when queues are managed fairly
•	Scale better for complex process trees
________________________________________
This presents it as a concept/architecture rather than claiming it is already a finished replacement for mutexes.

